  "fields": {
    "title": "Rhizome",
    "textContent": {
      "nodeType": "document",
      "data": {},
      "content": [
        {
          "nodeType": "heading-3",
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": "Description",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "nodeType": "paragraph",
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": "The new order of the Internet, in which everything can be linked to everything else, can be understood as a methaphor of the rhizome – a postmodern or poststructuralist model developed by the two philosophers Gilles Deleuze and Félix Guattari, which is based on non-hierarchical structures. ",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "nodeType": "blockquote",
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "nodeType": "paragraph",
              "data": {},
              "content": [
                {
                  "nodeType": "text",
                  "value": "»A rhizome has no beginning or end; it is always in the middle, between things, interbeing, intermezzo. The tree is filiation, but the rhizome is alliance, uniquely alliance.« ",
                  "marks": [],
                  "data": {}
                },
                {
                  "nodeType": "entry-hyperlink",
                  "data": {
                    "target": {
                      "sys": {
                        "id": "3pKvOdkEGGgxE7FgC80Z7c",
                        "type": "Link",
                        "linkType": "Entry"
                      }
                    }
                  },
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "nodeType": "text",
                      "value": "[1]",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "superscript"
                        }
                      ],
                      "data": {}
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "nodeType": "text",
                  "value": "",
                  "marks": [],
                  "data": {}
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "nodeType": "embedded-asset-block",
          "data": {
            "target": {
              "sys": {
                "id": "2qkSldpBsJZD64JnqW0DLX",
                "type": "Link",
                "linkType": "Asset"
              }
            }
          },
          "content": []
        },
        {
          "nodeType": "paragraph",
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": "The idea of the rhizome is a structural metaphor from botany and describes underground continuous growing roots that establish connections with other sprouts. A fundamental aspect of the symbol is that any point of the rhizome can be connected to any other point at any time.",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "entry-hyperlink",
              "data": {
                "target": {
                  "sys": {
                    "id": "1zhgcszVWEL6BKjSWq0zuC",
                    "type": "Link",
                    "linkType": "Entry"
                  }
                }
              },
              "content": [
                {
                  "nodeType": "text",
                  "value": "[2]",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "superscript"
                    }
                  ],
                  "data": {}
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": " Using the name of the Internet as a network or web, a structure is implied by definition in which any other point can be connected to any other point. Wherever users are and call up a URL in the browser, the link to the hyperspace is created and thus a new connection is established among billions of other already existing connections. Every single page on the Internet is just a few clicks away from every other page. The Internet, as a space of multiple multiplicities, in constant growth and change, is constantly forming new connections. Even when links and connections are broken or deleted, a new infinite number of interlocking nodes can emerge because there is no central point. Within a rhizome, new connections are constantly being made between elements – its elements are changeable, separable, connectable and reversible. These connections are made possible by servers that exist in several different locations around the world and are interconnected in a continuous manner, also creating new connections among themselves. It becomes clear that the nonlinear structure of the Internet is not only a rhizome, it also reproduces a rhizome.",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "entry-hyperlink",
              "data": {
                "target": {
                  "sys": {
                    "id": "1YyDBscttdWxMM5fpVDwaI",
                    "type": "Link",
                    "linkType": "Entry"
                  }
                }
              },
              "content": [
                {
                  "nodeType": "text",
                  "value": "[3]",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "superscript"
                    }
                  ],
                  "data": {}
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": "",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
